---
/**
 * The hero comparison: two simulated sessions doing the same job.
 *
 * WHY A SESSION RATHER THAN A PAIR OF CALLS
 *
 * A previous revision of this file put `"f creds get prod.db"` beside a JSON tool_use
 * block and called it a comparison. It was not one. The model writes about five tokens
 * either way, so that layout compared the only part of the two systems that is
 * identical and concealed the part that is not: what rides along in the body on every
 * turn. Cost in an agent session is a function of turns, and a one-line sample cannot
 * express that.
 *
 * WHY CACHING IS SHOWN AND NOT BURIED
 *
 * Because the honest number is much smaller than the impressive one. Dividing MCP's
 * per-turn definitions by f's index gives 35x. It should not: MCP tool definitions are
 * the first block of the body, byte-identical across every session of a given server
 * against a given provider, so they are a shared cache prefix — discounted to 10% of
 * base on an Anthropic cache read, and hit from request one. f's discovery output is a
 * tool_result inside message history, which is not a prefix, differs per session, and
 * is re-billed at full rate forever. The ratio on billed tokens is 3.8x. The ratio on
 * context-window occupancy, which caching does not touch, is 2877 tokens against 57.
 *
 * Those are the two numbers below. A hero that printed only the second one would be
 * choosing the flattering statistic, which is how the site's other figures got written
 * into Measurements with the caveats attached rather than the headlines.
 *
 * WHAT IS EXCLUDED, BOTH SIDES
 *
 * System prompt, user text, and the model's own prose: identical in shape on both
 * paths, and not what differs. Tool results are counted because they differ — MCP's
 * search came back "No matches found", which is 20 tokens of nothing, and f's came
 * back with content.
 *
 * WHAT THIS IS NOT
 *
 * A trace from a model. The turns are a plausible sequence (discover, look, read),
 * assembled from real tool definitions, real tool results and real `f` output — every
 * token figure below is a measurement of a real payload, but the decision to make
 * those three calls is authored. Do not describe this page as a benchmark transcript.
 *
 * THE ABSOLUTE COLUMN, AND WHY BOTH ARE SHOWN
 *
 * Tool payload alone is the honest unit for "what does a capability cost", but it is a
 * terrible unit for scale: 57 tokens of index inside a 9k-token request is not the
 * impression the bare number gives. So the second figure is the whole request body as
 * the server counted it -- scripts/measure-absolute.mjs runs this job twice against a
 * real model with identical system prompt, identical shell tool and identical result
 * framing, and reads prompt_tokens off the response. That framing is the provider's
 * own and cannot be reconstructed from a local rank, which is why it is measured rather
 * than estimated.
 *
 * The absolute ratio is much smaller than the payload ratio, and that is the finding,
 * not a caveat: most of a real request is framing both sides pay. What the schema adds
 * is the 2,202-token difference between the two first turns -- everything else is what
 * any agent costs regardless of how capabilities arrive.
 *
 * Numbers: scripts/measure-session.mjs (payload, o200k_base) and
 * scripts/measure-absolute.mjs (whole body, server-counted).
 */

// From scripts/measure-session.mjs. Re-run it and edit what it prints.
const MCP = {
  server: "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem",
  tools: 14,
  defsPerTurn: 2877,
  resultTurns: [24, 46], // search_files, read_text_file
  results: 70,
  uncached: 8701,
  cached: 933,
  // Whole request body, counted by the server, one turn per exchange.
  body: [2579, 2601, 2622, 2648],
};

const F = {
  // 57 tokens is the measured index for the store this ran against; the uck count goes
  // with it from the same snapshot rather than being quoted from memory -- /ucks/ and
  // /docs/measurements/ both cite 61 tokens / 16 ucks, and the hero must not disagree.
  ucks: 16,
  indexOnce: 57,
  indexResent: 57,
  search: 21,
  resultTurns: [42, 14], // creds status, creds get (miss)
  results: 56,
  total: 248,
  body: [377, 408, 439, 463],
};

const RATIO = { cached: 3.8, uncached: 35.1 };

/* One divisor for both panes, derived from the larger series. Scaling each column to
   its own maximum would make two bars of very different height look equal-heighted,
   which is the specific way a chart becomes a lie. */
const BAR_MAX = Math.max(...MCP.body, ...F.body);
const barPx = (t) => Math.max(4, Math.round((t / BAR_MAX) * 132));
---

<div class="compare">
  <!-- The job, stated once, because "same cost" only means anything against a task. -->
  <p class="compare-job">
    Three turns against one credential: discover what exists, look for the value, read it.
    Each row is that turn's tool payload; the bars below are the <b>whole request body</b>
    counted by a real model, so the ratio is read against a real window rather than
    against nothing.
  </p>

  <div class="compare-panes">
    <section class="compare-col" aria-label="Session using MCP tools">
      <p class="compare-head">
        <span class="compare-svc">MCP</span>
        <span class="compare-sub">{MCP.tools} filesystem tools registered</span>
      </p>

      <div class="trace">
        <div class="trace-turn">
          <span class="tt">1</span>
          <code class="tl">tools/list → 14 definitions</code>
          <span class="tn">{MCP.defsPerTurn.toLocaleString()}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="trace-turn">
          <span class="tt">2</span>
          <code class="tl">search_files → "No matches found"</code>
          <span class="tn">{MCP.resultTurns[0]}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="trace-turn">
          <span class="tt">3</span>
          <code class="tl">read_text_file → contents</code>
          <span class="tn">{MCP.resultTurns[1]}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="trace-sum">
          definitions ride on all three: <b>{(MCP.defsPerTurn * 3).toLocaleString()}</b>
          + results {MCP.results} = <b>{MCP.uncached.toLocaleString()}</b>
        </div>
        <div class="trace-body">
          <span>whole body, turn 1 → 4</span>
          <span class="bars">
            {MCP.body.map((t, i) => (
              <span class="bar" style={`--h:${barPx(t)}px`} title={`turn ${i + 1}: ${t} tokens`}>
                <span class="bar-v">{t.toLocaleString()}</span>
              </span>
            ))}
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="compare-foot">
        <b>{MCP.cached.toLocaleString()}</b> billed once the definition prefix caches
        (Anthropic reads at 10% of base; it is byte-identical across sessions, so the
        first request already hits it).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="compare-col" aria-label="Session using f">
      <p class="compare-head">
        <span class="compare-svc f">f</span>
        <span class="compare-sub">no tool definitions</span>
      </p>

      <div class="trace">
        <div class="trace-turn">
          <span class="tt">1</span>
          <code class="tl">f → {F.ucks} uck names</code>
          <span class="tn">{F.indexOnce}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="trace-turn">
          <span class="tt">2</span>
          <code class="tl">f search credential</code>
          <span class="tn">{F.search}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="trace-turn">
          <span class="tt">3</span>
          <code class="tl">f creds status</code>
          <span class="tn">{F.resultTurns[0]}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="trace-sum">
          index re-sent each turn (it lives in history): <b>{(F.indexResent * 3)}</b>
          + calls <b>{F.search + F.results}</b> = <b>{F.total}</b>
        </div>
        <div class="trace-body">
          <span>whole body, turn 1 → 4</span>
          <span class="bars">
            {F.body.map((t, i) => (
              <span class="bar" style={`--h:${barPx(t)}px`} title={`turn ${i + 1}: ${t} tokens`}>
                <span class="bar-v">{t}</span>
              </span>
            ))}
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="compare-foot">
        <b>{F.total}</b> billed. Nothing to cache and nothing to invalidate — but the
        index is a <em>tool_result</em>, so it is re-billed at full rate on every request.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <!-- Both ratios, with the axes named. The smaller ratio is the honest one for billed
       tokens; occupancy is where the difference actually is. -->
  <p class="compare-verdict">
    The same request, counted by the server it was sent to:
    <b>{MCP.body[0].toLocaleString()} vs {F.body[0].toLocaleString()}</b> tokens on turn
    one — so the schema costs <b>{(MCP.body[0] - F.body[0]).toLocaleString()} tokens</b>
    over a framing both sides pay.
    <b>{RATIO.cached}×</b> on billed tool tokens, and
    <b>{MCP.defsPerTurn.toLocaleString()} vs {F.indexOnce}</b> of window occupied, which
    caching does not change.
    <a href="/docs/measurements/">how these were measured</a>
  </p>
</div>
